  if (A.quickMatch.length === QM.length) A.quickMatch.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, QM[i]); });

  A.pairingFood = "【Dark chocolate is the royal road】First among foods is dark chocolate (70% cacao or more). A small piece in the mouth lets its bitterness and the cigar's richness draw out each other's deeper flavours, and it is said to work especially well with full-bodied maduros and spicy Nicaraguans. Rich desserts such as chocolate mousse or flourless cake bring out the cacao notes of cigars of the Padrón 1964 or Oliva Serie V class. Milk chocolate lets sweetness win too easily; high-cacao and bitter is the rule.\n【Nuts】Dry-roasted or salted almonds, hazelnuts, pecans, walnuts and cashews are the safe bet that complements a cigar's nutty notes, considered versatile across most cigar types. Their salt cuts astringency and bite, resetting the palate and drawing out sweetness. The buttery sweetness of hazelnuts points up the hidden sweetness of a medium cigar, and walnuts, working on both the sweet and the bitter side, underline earth and wood.\n【Cheese】Match by maturity, as a rule. Creamy young cheeses such as Brie and Gouda suit mild cigars, their smooth texture keeping company with delicate aromas without covering them. Mature Cheddar, Parmigiano, cave-aged Gruyère (nutty, with spice) and the salt, umami and edge of blue cheese stand up to medium-to-full cigars.\n【Dried fruit and burnt sugar】The concentrated sweetness of dates, figs and raisins is ideal for bridging a cigar to the sweetness of PX or port, laying the ground for a contrast pairing. Burnt-sugar flavours — caramel, toffee, molasses — work complementarily with vanilla-and-oak cigars and with bourbon and rum.\n【Meat and salt】The salt and umami of cured ham and salami suit the earth and leather of a full cigar, and grilled red meat leads naturally into the after-dinner sequence of a full cigar with red wine.\n【The principle】The basics are to match each element — salt, bitter, sweet, umami — to the cigar's strength. Strong-flavoured dishes before or between smokes (garlic, heavy spices, raw citrus) overwrite a cigar's delicate aromatics, so the connoisseur's courtesy is to keep to a light plate with the cigar as protagonist, tidying the palate with water as you go. Pairing is a search for the balance in which 'the food flatters the cigar yet shines in its own right' — there is no single right answer.";

  A.pairingTime = "Varying the strength of cigar and drink by time of day reflects the way palate and condition change over the day — a rule of thumb many aficionados share from experience.\n【Morning】After waking the palate is gentle, so a mild-to-medium claro with coffee (blonde to medium roast) is the golden rule. A Dominican cigar with a blonde roast has a lightness that could be enjoyed all day. But pairing a strong cigar or high alcohol on an empty stomach speeds the absorption of nicotine and readily brings on nicotine sickness ('nic sick' — nausea, dizziness, sweating, palpitations), so avoid it. Eating a little beforehand slows absorption and steadies blood sugar.\n【Afternoon】A medium cigar with black tea or oolong, a lighter rum or a reposado tequila, or an IPA sits comfortably. An Americano with a medium Dominican cigar — full of woody aroma, untiring and satisfying — is regarded as 'the mid-afternoon standard'. For a post-meal smoke, tawny port or sherry keeps gentle company and suits the hour of digestion.\n【Early evening】To mark the turn of the day, a medium to medium-full cigar with bourbon, a VSOP-to-XO cognac or a sherry-cask Scotch makes the bridge. If dinner is still to come, have a light bite to avoid an empty stomach.\n【Night, after dinner】With the palate sated by a meal, this is the best hour for receiving full bodies. A full-bodied maduro with Islay or sherry-cask Scotch, XO cognac or Armagnac, aged dark rum, PX sherry, stout or mezcal are the classic closers. In the cigar's final third, as the flavours intensify, cognac's smooth finish and sweet fortified wines show especially well.\n【Care throughout】Slowly smoking a long vitola (Churchill or double corona) belongs to the evening, when the palate has settled. Factor nicotine tolerance, hunger, temperature and the day's condition into the choice of strength, and keep the unhurried pace of one puff every 45 seconds to a minute and a half. Smoking back to back gives the body no interval to clear its nicotine and invites sickness, so leave a gap between cigars and never let hydration lapse — that is held to be the key to enjoying them safely.";

  A.pairingNg = "①【Strength mismatch (crushing)】The most common failure. Set heavy Islay peat, a dense full-bodied red or the intense sweetness of PX against a delicate mild cigar and its aromas are completely buried, rendering the exercise pointless. Light drinks with light cigars is the iron rule.\n②【Peat is for the experienced only】Islay smoke competes readily with cigar smoke: pair it only with full-bodied maduros. Set against a cigar of middling strength, both characters muddy and cancel each other out.\n③【High proof without water】Cask-strength whisky or a high-proof bourbon taken neat numbs the palate and overwhelms the cigar. The standard move is a few drops of water to soften the bite and open closed aromas. Going in neat is only for full cigars of matching strength.\n④【Empty stomach, strong cigar, strong drink】The classic trigger for nicotine sickness. Hunger speeds the absorption of nicotine, and alcohol amplifies its effects through dehydration and falling blood sugar. Caffeine with nicotine can heighten the sickness too. A light meal and water beforehand are the prevention.\n⑤【Bitterness amplified by lack of sugar】A bone-dry red wine or a dry spirit can over-resonate with an astringent cigar — tannin against bitterness — and turn metallic. Balance it with the sweetness of port, sherry or rum.\n⑥【Strong-flavoured mixers and lingering food aromas】Cola, artificial flavourings, strong mint or citrus, and dishes heavy with garlic or spices overwrite the cigar's delicate aromatics — avoid them.\n⑦【Drinks that are too cold】A heavily chilled drink or ice closes the aromas and dulls the palate; room temperature to lightly chilled is the rule.\n⑧【Smoking too fast】Rushing makes the leaf burn fast and sends the nicotine surging all at once, ruining even the best pairing. Smoke slowly, and take a sip after each puff to reset the mouth.\n⑨【Ignoring palate fatigue】Piling strong spirits or dark coffee one on another saturates the palate, and you stop catching the cigar's changes in its second half. Slip in some water (room temperature) between to reset tongue and nose, and the resolution holds to the end.\n⑩【Dessert-grade sweetness on a light cigar】Set PX, an intensely sweet port or a sweet liqueur against a mild cigar and only the sweetness remains — the cigar vanishes entirely. Strong sweetness must always be used in contrast against the bitterness and earth of a full-bodied maduro.\n※Pairing is a matter of taste, and these are general guidelines rather than absolute prohibitions. A deliberate 'miss' aimed at contrast can produce something delightful, so in the end, testing little by little with your own palate is held to be the quickest way to improve.";

  A.rhScience = "【RH and equilibrium moisture content】Relative humidity equilibrates with the moisture held inside a cigar, and that moisture content is the single most important variable, governing burn rate, draw and aroma alike. Cigars leave the factory finished at roughly 12–15% moisture by weight, the accepted proper range in which suppleness and even burning coexist.\n【The idea of EMC】Wood and leaf take up and give off moisture in response to the surrounding temperature and humidity, eventually settling at a balance point called the equilibrium moisture content (EMC). A cigar likewise follows the box's RH and converges on its EMC over days to weeks, so holding your target RH 'steadily' matters more than any one-off reading.\n【Aiming at 65%】Taut, dry-toned and sharp-burning. The wrapper is drawn tight, the cigar lights readily, burns quickly and holds a firm ash. Wrapper-derived aromas come through at their clearest, and mould risk is at its lowest. Thick, oily wrappers such as maduros are in fact said to show their best at around 65%.\n【69–70%】The standard zone. Moist, easy to handle and an all-round range in which the three layers harmonise, though the burn slows somewhat and relights begin to increase. It is also the safe 'set it and forget it' landing point that many manufacturers recommend.\n【The danger above 72%】Rich, oily and dense, but the risks of a plugged draw, uneven burning and the cigar going out all increase. Above 75% in particular, mould (blue-green and fuzzy, hard to remove by wiping) grows readily. Below 62%, conversely, the wrapper dries out and cracks easily — the lower bound calls for the same caution.\n【Signs of over-humidification】Spongy softness when squeezed, a heavy draw, burning down one side ('canoeing') or only up the core ('tunnelling'), frequent self-extinguishing, sour heavy smoke. These are the classic marks of excess moisture, and often mean the box's RH is running too high.\n【Bringing it back down】Remove the humidification media, open the lid a few centimetres for about 30 minutes once a day to change the air, and lower the RH slowly, by 1–2% per day. Sudden drying invites cracked wrappers and split rolls, so moisture must move out on the same gentle gradient as it moves in — that is the iron rule.\n【Temperature and RH】In the physics of dry air, RH falls as temperature rises; but in a real humidor holding humidification media, a rise in temperature increases evaporation and pushes RH upwards. At the same 70% RH, air at 26°C carries far more absolute moisture than air at 21°C and is held far riskier.\n【Monitoring and calibration】Hygrometers drift by a few per cent from unit to unit, so never take them on trust. Calibrate first by the saturated-salt method (table salt wetted with a little water to damp sand, sealed in a container for 6–24 hours, reads about 75%) and then read. Measuring at several points in the box also reveals any unevenness.\n【Go digital】Analogue bimetal hygrometers drift with age and carry large errors. Use a digital unit of the ±1% class, and verify its accuracy regularly with a calibration kit or the saturated-salt method — that is the reliable way.\n【The smoke tells you too】At proper humidity the smoke is clear and sweet; too dry and it turns hot and harsh; too damp and it turns heavy and sour. Beyond the box's readings, the quality of the smoke itself is diagnostic material.\n【Routine checks】Check the hygrometer about once a week, and the humidification media about once a month for hardening or spills. A Boveda that has gone from soft gel to hard and stiff is signalling replacement; depending on the environment its life is put at a few months to half a year.\n【Capacity and fill】A box with much empty space forces the media to humidify a large volume and the RH runs unstable. Filling it to about 70–80% with cigars or packing stabilises the environment and slows the media's consumption.\n【The dry box】Leaf too damp to burn well can be 'dry-boxed' in an empty wooden box for 24–48 hours, dropping its moisture by 5–10 points; the draw firms up, the burn steadies, and hidden sweetness and flavour are said to emerge.\n【Summer and winter in practice】In summer, bring the temperature down with a wineador or the like; in winter, add media so heating-dried air does not win. Even the same box needs seasonal handling — it can never simply be left to a setting decided once.\n【The ideal range】High temperature is bound up with the triple risk of high RH, mould and tobacco beetles. The ideal is roughly 16–21°C and 65–72% RH. More than any particular number, 'steady operation without swings' is the heart of storage.";

  A.rhDebate = "【The fall of the 70/70 myth】The old standard rule of '70% RH at 70°F (21°C)' is now widely regarded as tending damp and behind the times. 70% is not fatal, but the burn dulls, relights multiply and mould risk climbs — 'the cigar never shows its full ability' is the connoisseur's rebuttal.\n【Where 70/70 came from】The figure originally stems from instruction slips American manufacturers put in their boxes, and spread rapidly among retailers and customers because it was easy to remember — a marketing-born rule of thumb. It is also a number that mirrors the environment of tropical producing countries such as Cuba and the Dominican Republic.\n【Regional differences】70/70 is chiefly a North American custom; European and Asian aficionados have long kept their cigars at lower temperatures and humidities. It is not 'the one right answer' but one standard rooted in region and culture.\n【The case for 65–69%】The recent consensus of the community is that 65–69% RH at 16–21°C gives a crisper burn, a sharper draw and clearer aromas. Aficionados in cool regions, and in humid ones, lean strongly towards 65–67%.\n【The manufacturers' drift】Boveda has made 69% its mainstay (standard recommendation), and the industry now presents a window of '65–72% RH, 64–70°F' as the acceptable range. The movement is away from fixation on a single number and towards stability within that window.\n【An optimum that moves with climate】The optimum shifts with climate, marque and taste. Slightly higher in arid regions, slightly lower in humid ones; higher for thick, oily wrappers, lower for thin ones — so the guide runs. Bear in mind too that the same 72% RH grows far riskier as the temperature climbs.\n【Fine-tuning to taste】The advanced approach is to watch draw and burn as you smoke and adjust to your own environment and leaf. If things run damp, drop a few per cent or use the dry box; if dry, add media and bring it back.\n【Stability above all】Whichever RH you choose, a box that stays constant — even a touch low — treats the leaf better than one swinging several per cent a day. The destination is not the hunt for a number but the suppression of variation.\n【How to settle it】In the end, judge by your own cigar. Smoke the same marque at RHs a few per cent apart and find the point where draw, burn and aroma line up best — the quickest way of checking the answer.\n【The conversion trap】Humidity depends on temperature, so a hygrometer reading taken in the room must not be applied as-is as the target inside the box. Always measure inside the box, in conditions close to the target temperature, before judging.\n【The seasoned consensus】In short, '70% is a ceiling, not a target'. Build your baseline at 69% first, then, watching your own leaf and environment, work downwards a few per cent at a time in search of the optimum — that is held to be the standard route.";

  A.spanishCedar = "【What it actually is】Spanish cedar (Cedrela odorata), despite the name, is no true cedar but a hardwood of the same family as mahogany (the Meliaceae); 'Spanish cedar' is a common name. Native to Central and South America, it has long been used in Cuba as box and packing material for cigars.\n【1: Humidity buffering】Moderately porous, it takes up excess moisture when the box's RH rises above target and releases it when RH falls below — a natural buffer. It smooths the sudden humidity swings caused by opening the lid or by changes in room temperature. The heart of its affinity with cigars is precisely that it breathes moisture in and out just as they do.\n【2: Pest deterrence】Its essential oils — chiefly cedrol, with α-cedrene and others — are held to repel the tobacco beetle. This is deterrence, not complete protection, and as the oils fade with the years the effect weakens. Once the scent has gone, understand that the protective power has faded with it.\n【3: Aroma and maturation】Cedar's aromatic compounds interact with a cigar's oils over the long term, lending notes of sweet wood, cedar and hazelnut, and complexity. Long ageing is said to deepen the effect further.\n【4: Stability of the wood】It resists warping and cracking even in humid conditions, and is light and easy to work. That is exactly why it has been considered the ideal lining for humidors.\n【Drying is a precondition】Stock not kiln-dried or properly rested before use seeps the wood's moisture and resin and disturbs flavour. Hence the necessity of selecting good stock and of seasoning.\n【Against the alternatives】Honduran mahogany absorbs moisture much as cedar does, with a milder scent and a lower price, but is held inferior in pest deterrence and aroma. American red cedar deters pests and mould but smells too strong, with many reporting harm to flavour. Clear acrylic is decorative, for showing the contents, and does not buffer humidity. All told, Spanish cedar is the first choice — and priced accordingly.\n【Reviving dried cedar】A lining dried out by long disuse loses buffering power, but re-seasoning the box largely restores the function. The oil-borne pest deterrence and fragrance, though, are lost gradually with age and never fully return — that much should be understood.\n【Cedar and mould】Cedar has some mould resistance but is no cure-all: over-humidify, or use tap water, and mould will grow on cedar too. Its buffering merely softens sudden swings in humidity — it is no substitute for proper humidification and clean housekeeping.\n【The scent fades】Even the strong cedar scent of a new box mellows gradually over a few years. The buffering function remains after the scent thins, but the deterrent and aromatising powers decline; if needed, thin cedar sheets can be added to make up the loss.\n【Other hardwoods】African mahogany and others appear on occasion, but by the tradition and track record running back to Cuba, Spanish cedar keeps its place as the standard.\n【Sheets and spills】Cuban cabinets wrap their cigars in thin cedar sheets for humidity control and pest deterrence. There is also the connoisseur's practice of lighting with a thin 'cedar spill' when smoking, avoiding gas or oil odours while lending a trace of cedar aroma.\n【Telling the real thing】Fresh stock is a pinkish red-brown, gives off the distinctive fragrance, and is comparatively light. Authenticity and quality bear directly on buffering, pest deterrence and flavour, so the provenance of the lining is no element to take lightly. Poorly dyed stock, or wood still holding resin, disturbs flavour — choose a box from a maker you can trust.";

  A.seasoning = "【What seasoning is】Seasoning is the preparatory step of gradually charging the wood of a dry new humidor with moisture, so that the box no longer robs the interior of RH once cigars go in. Put cigars into an unseasoned box and the wood draws up their moisture, drying and cracking them — hence the step is essential.\n【Why the wood steals water】Dry wood craves moisture more strongly than the cigars do, so leaf placed in an unseasoned box has its water drawn out of it. Saturate the wood thoroughly first, and the box will no longer rob the cigars of their moisture.\n【No hurrying】Give in to the temptation to load cigars into a half-finished box and the wood pulls water from the leaf, which dries and cracks within days. Seasoning is the very foundation of successful storage; skip it and everything that follows collapses.\n【The great rule: no water on the wood】Pouring water directly onto the wood, or soaking it, is strictly forbidden. Direct contact with liquid raises the grain, which can snag wrappers, and invites a 'sham seasoning' — damp on the surface, dry at the core — as well as mould. Whatever water you use must be distilled.\n【Why distilled water】Tap water carries minerals and chlorine, plus bacteria and mould spores; it clogs the fine pores of humidification media, leaves white residue on the wood, and encourages mould. This single point is an iron rule common to every method.\n【Method A: Boveda 84% (recommended)】Place dedicated B84 seasoning packs in the empty box and close the lid; the vapour (the gas phase) carries moisture slowly to the core of the wood. Two packs per 50-cigar capacity is the guide, and the iron rule is to wait 14 days without opening. When it is done, discard the packs and switch to your everyday 69% packs.\n【Method B: distilled water and a sponge (faster, but needs watching)】Wring out a lint-free cloth wetted with distilled water until firm, and wipe the interior walls to 'damp but not dripping'. Place a dish of distilled water and the humidification media inside, close the lid and check daily. Most boxes reach about 70% RH in 5–7 days.\n【Size changes the timetable】The larger, newer and drier the wood, the more moisture it craves and the longer it takes. A small desktop finishes in one to two weeks; a large cabinet can take several weeks. Do not rush — wait for the wood to saturate.\n【Even new boxes vary】Units of the same product differ in how dry the wood is and how tight the lid seals, so finishing times vary. Judge not by the calendar guide but by the established fact of a stable hygrometer — that is the reliable course.\n【How many everyday packs】For the everyday two-way packs used at the finish, the guide is one 60g pack per 25 cigars of capacity. Too few packs for the volume and the RH responds sluggishly, so larger boxes need several.\n【Beware overdoing it】Season too far, with the interior held well above 75%, and you have a breeding ground for mould. Stop once the target RH is reached and switch to the everyday media. In seasoning, 'too much' is as much a failure as 'not enough'.\n【Calibrate the hygrometer first】Calibrating the hygrometer by the saturated-salt method (about 75%) before and after seasoning lets you judge correctly whether the box is truly done. Trusting an uncalibrated reading and loading cigars early is a common way to fail.\n【Re-seasoning】After a mould outbreak, evacuate the cigars, wipe the interior with isopropyl alcohol, dry and air it for at least 24 hours, then season the box afresh. A box dried out by long neglect likewise needs re-seasoning.\n【The final check】Whichever method you use, confirm at the end with a digital hygrometer that the RH holds steady for several days before the cigars go in. Not begrudging the effort is, in the end, the shortest route.";

