  A.beetles = "【The greatest enemy】The tobacco beetle (cigar beetle, Lasioderma serricorne) is the cigar's greatest enemy: eggs laid on the leaf in the field before harvest hatch in heat and humidity and eat their way through. A red-brown beetle 2–3mm long, whose eggs can lurk even in premium cigars.\n【Why premium cigars are targeted】The more a premium cigar relies on natural leaf without harsh insecticidal treatment, the more readily eggs survive. In cheap machine-made cigars the heat and processing of manufacture have usually killed the eggs — ironically, the finer the leaf, the greater the risk it carries.\n【Conditions for an outbreak】Eggs are held to hatch and become active when temperature exceeds roughly 22°C (72°F) and RH exceeds 72%, so keeping the temperature below that threshold is the first line of prevention. Research too shows that growth and breeding speed depend strongly on temperature — generations turn over faster in heat — making high temperature the greatest trigger of all.\n【Life cycle】A female lays 10–100 eggs; larvae hatch in roughly 6–10 days, feed for 5–10 weeks through several instars, then pupate; the pupal stage lasts 1–3 weeks and the adult lives 1–4 weeks. A full generation is put at roughly 40–90 days.\n【The signs】Small holes 1–2mm across in the cigar's surface or about the box, powder, and small beetles on the move. Find even one, quarantine the entire stock and move to treatment at once. Left alone, they can wipe out a box within days.\n【Prevention first】The best defence is temperature control: hold the box below 22°C and hatching itself is suppressed. Use a wineador or the like to escape summer heat, and inspect with particular care after any exposure to high temperature — a heatwave, a power cut, a move. The deterrent effect of a cedar lining helps too.\n【Hold humidity down too】Keeping RH below 72% on top of the temperature control further suppresses hatching and activity. Heat and damp arriving together is the worst condition of all; cut off either one alone and the risk falls sharply.\n【With large stocks or no equipment】One approach is first to confirm that a trusted retailer freezes stock on arrival. At home, divide the cigars into batches and freeze them in turn. Above all, an environment held cool (below 22°C) shuts off outbreaks at the source.\n【Freezing: the cooling steps】1. Seal the cigars in zip-lock bags (doubled and sealed). 2. Do not freeze at once: pre-chill in the refrigerator for about 24 hours to soften the temperature difference. 3. Freeze for at least 72 hours (three days). At −18°C for 72 hours, adults, larvae and eggs alike are held to be killed.\n【Freezing: the thawing steps】4. On removal, thaw gently in the refrigerator again over about 24 hours. 5. Finally return to room temperature. An abrupt temperature difference cracks the wrapper through sudden expansion, so always proceed in stages. 6. Before restocking, clean the humidor and remove every trace of insects, eggs and powder.\n【On discovery, isolate at once】Once found, move the suspect boxes out of the humidor in sealed bags, keep them apart from the rest, and send them promptly for freezing. Empty and clean the box, re-season it if need be, and cut off any seed of recurrence.\n【The essentials】The gentle temperature gradient of 'pre-chill → deep freeze → refrigerator thaw → room temperature' is the key to preventing cracks. Industry also uses fumigation with phosphine and the like, but at home staged freezing is held the practical and reliable extermination. Prevention (keeping cool) and early detection are the best defence of all.";

  A.longAging = "【What ageing is】Long ageing is the process in which residual ammonia from fermentation dissipates, the oils (oleoresins) and moisture of the three layers migrate and even out across the leaves, and the sharp edges of youth (ammonia, green notes, harshness) round into complexity.\n【The chemistry of change】During maturation, resins and essential oils break down and recombine into smaller molecules, and the flavours melt together into a unified whole. As irritants such as ammonia evaporate, the 'bite' on the tongue recedes, and delicate aromas once hidden behind stronger flavours are said to come to the fore.\n【When it begins】After rolling, cigars customarily rest at the factory for at least 90 days or so to let the leaves settle together (the marriage) before shipping. Ageing at home after purchase is the continuation of that process — it does not begin from zero.\n【Short, medium, long】Very roughly, the stages run: under a year is the 'rest', as the ammonia clears; at one to three years the edges come off and things harmonise; at three to five years the complexity blossoms. Progress varies greatly with marque and leaf quality.\n【When they come good】Most improve markedly at two to five years, and many voices favour around three years for complex blends. Heavyweight Nicaraguans, Hondurans and Havanas have the constitution for five to ten years, but the peak for most leaf is placed at two to five.\n【The risk of over-ageing】Continued 'improvement' beyond five to seven years is rare and exceptional. Go too far and the oils wither; aroma and power thin out and go flat. Lighter leaf fades sooner — 'age everything' is not the way.\n【Leaf better left unaged】Mild leaf, infused or flavoured leaf, and leaf delicate from the start tend to lose character or shed aroma over long ageing. Such cigars are held best enjoyed young and fresh.\n【What tends to blossom】Full-bodied, oil-rich leaf of high quality gains most over the long haul. The names that recur are Arturo Fuente (OpusX/Añejo), Padrón 1926, Davidoff and Ashton VSG, and many Havana leaves are the classics that flower with age.\n【The ageing environment】Ageing is storage itself, and a stable, lowish environment of about 65–70% RH at 16–18°C is held to suit it. Heat and damp bring not maturation but mould and beetles — a quiet, dark, constant-temperature setting is the ideal.\n【Cellophane and tubes】For long ageing, removing the cellophane and the aluminium, glass or wooden tubes and resting the cigars naked lets them exchange oils and aromas within the box and advances maturation. Cellophane, on the other hand, being semi-permeable, creates a microclimate that lets ageing proceed gently — so for 15-to-20-year projects there is also a school that leaves it on for protection. Cellophane stained amber over the years ('yellow cello') is the mark of good ageing; for carrying and short-term storage, leaving it on to protect the wrapper is the safe course.\n【Checks and rotation】Even in long-term storage, inspect the humidity and check for beetles every few months. Rotating boxes from the lower and rear positions to the top and front evens out the environment, and helps avoid the failure of forgetting a box into over-age.\n【Plume versus mould】The white powdery plume (bloom) that appears in long storage is crystallised oil, the mark of maturation, harmless and brushed away with a finger. It is quite distinct from mould — blue-green, fuzzy, not brushing off — and one needs the eye not to throw away good cigars by confusing the two.\n【Impatience is the greatest enemy】There is no short cut in ageing, and every opening to check disturbs the environment. Good leaf, a stable box, and quietly entrusting it to time is the only method; the posture required is to watch over it by the year.\n【Box ageing and the marriage】Ageing whole cabinets or dress boxes — 'box ageing' — is the standard: within the box the cigars mature together as one, their moisture and aromas homogenising. Leaves long housed together transfer and unify their aromas, but house a strongly scented cigar with a delicate one and both are spoiled — segregating storage by strength of aroma is the iron rule.";

